.\"
.\" UNLINK(2) - Substrate Native Personality
.\"
.TH UNLINK 2 "2026-09-29" "Substrate OS" "System Calls"
.SH NAME
unlink, unlinkat \- remove a directory entry
.SH LIBRARY
Standard C library
.RI ( libc ,\~ \-lc )
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <unistd.h>
.PP
.BI "int unlink(const char *" path );
.PP
.BR "#include <fcntl.h>" "           /* Definition of AT_* constants */"
.B #include <unistd.h>
.PP
.BI "int unlinkat(int " dirfd ", const char *" path ", int " flags );
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR unlink ()
removes the directory entry
.I path
and decrements the link count of the file it names.
When the count reaches zero and no process has the file open, the file's
storage is released; if a process still has it open, the storage is
released when the last descriptor is closed.
If
.I path
names a symbolic link, the link itself is removed, not the file it
points to.
.PP
.BR unlinkat ()
does the same, except that a relative
.I path
is resolved against the directory open on
.I dirfd
(or the current directory if
.I dirfd
is
.BR AT_FDCWD ),
and that
.I flags
may be
.BR AT_REMOVEDIR ,
in which case
.I path
must name an empty directory, which is removed as by
.BR rmdir (2).
.PP
Removing an entry requires write and search permission on the directory
that contains it.
If that directory has the sticky bit set
.RB ( S_ISVTX ),
an unprivileged caller may only remove entries for files it owns or in
a directory it owns.
.SH RETURN VALUE
On success, 0 is returned.
On error, \-1 is returned and
.I errno
is set to indicate the error.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EACCES
Write permission is denied on the directory containing
.IR path ,
or search permission is denied on a directory in the path prefix.
.TP
.B EBADF
.RB ( unlinkat ())
.I path
is relative and
.I dirfd
is neither
.B AT_FDCWD
nor an open descriptor.
.TP
.B EFAULT
.I path
points outside the accessible address space.
.TP
.B EINVAL
.I path
is empty, or
.RB ( unlinkat ())
.I flags
contains a bit other than
.BR AT_REMOVEDIR .
.TP
.B EISDIR
.I path
names a directory (use
.BR rmdir (2)
or
.B AT_REMOVEDIR
instead).
.TP
.B ENAMETOOLONG
.I path
is longer than 255 bytes.
.TP
.B ENOENT
A component of
.I path
does not exist, or
.I path
does not exist.
.TP
.B ELOOP
Too many symbolic links were met resolving the path prefix.
.TP
.B ENOTDIR
A component of the path prefix is not a directory; or
.RB ( unlinkat ())
.I path
is relative and
.I dirfd
refers to a file other than a directory; or
.B AT_REMOVEDIR
was given and
.I path
does not name a directory.
.TP
.B EPERM
The containing directory has the sticky bit set and the caller owns
neither the file nor the directory, and is not privileged; or the file
system does not allow entries to be removed (devfs, procfs, sysfs).
.TP
.B EROFS
The entry is on a file system mounted read-only.
.SH NOTES
.SS Substrate Native ABI
.nf
unlink:    Syscall Number: 10 (SYS_UNLINK)
           Arguments:      const char *path
unlinkat:  Syscall Number: 301 (SYS_UNLINKAT)
           Arguments:      int dirfd, const char *path, int flags
Returns:   0, or \-errno
.fi
.SH EXAMPLE
Create a scratch file, keep using it, and make sure its name is gone
even if the program is killed:
.PP
.EX
#include <fcntl.h>
#include <unistd.h>

int
scratch_fd(void)
{
    int fd = open("/tmp/scratch", O_RDWR | O_CREAT | O_EXCL, 0600);

    if (fd >= 0)
        unlink("/tmp/scratch");   /* storage lives until close(fd) */
    return fd;
}
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR rm (1),
.BR close (2),
.BR link (2),
.BR rename (2),
.BR rmdir (2),
.BR symlink (2)
.SH STANDARDS
POSIX.1-2024.
